# close
## The steps
The court breaks up, as a court does, in small knots and slow waves, with a great deal of low conversation in which your name goes round like a coin. You are presented. You are bowed to. You are given a cup, and decline it, and are given another, and take it. You are told, by a lady with a face like a moon and a smile like a sharp knife, that she knew a man who had a scar just like yours. You say that it is a very common scar. She says that it is the only one that she has ever seen on a living face.
?if _vieri == 1
  Vieri Salimbeni brings you a message. It is on very thin paper, folded very small, and it is not from him.
?else
  A page in the Merchant blue brings you a message. It is on very thin paper, folded very small, and he does not look at your face.
?end
It is in a hand as quick and level as an adding machine.
> *Tomorrow, an hour after dark. The strong room, not the parlor. Come alone.*
> *P.*
?if b2.seat == "sword"
  There is, underneath, in the same hand, a smaller line, written afterwards, in a different ink. *I did ask you not to.*
?end
You go out, at last, by the long stair, with Ottaviano beside you in an attentive silence. At the foot of it, in the street, with the sky already turning the color of a drowned slate, he stops, and says very gravely that the Prince is grateful for the Signore's presence. He says it as one reads from a card. Then he adds, in a different voice, very low, that the Prince does not as a rule rise to greet a guest, and that in the whole of his time he has seen him do it twice.
You ask who the other was.
"I am not at liberty to say," says Ottaviano. He is very white. He looks at your hands. "But he was not so tall."
